fof_version: '1.0'
type: enemy
id: kindly_one
name: A Kindly One
ruleset:
  modules:
  - id: base
    version: 1.0.0
enemy:
  # v0.3: Named, durability 4, attack +0, rating 8.
  level: 2
  role: standard
  armor: 0
  morale: 2
  weapon: None. It is holding something out.
  wants: A fair trade, where fair means what a thing is worth to its owner.
  special: 'FAIR DEALING — it cannot decline a genuinely fair offer, and knows fair from unfair
    without being told: no roll, and the MM does not get to refuse on its behalf. Cheat it
    once and neither it nor any other Kindly One ever trades with that person again: no roll,
    and no apology that works.'
  when_bloodied: It leaves, whatever the morale roll said.
  tells: It spoke first, politely, in your language, and it has been holding the thing out
    for longer than anyone should be able to.
  breaks: It never strikes back. If struck it Defends (attacks on it are Hard) and leaves
    at the end of the exchange, and does not come back. Neither does any other Kindly One,
    anywhere, for the rest of the campaign.
  twists:
  - What it holds out is something the party will need later and cannot yet know they want.
  - It is holding out something a party member lost years ago.
  - Someone at the crossing already cheated one, and is trying to get the party to trade on
    their behalf.
  - It wants something nobody in the party thinks is worth anything, and it is right.
  - A local has taught the party the rhyme. The rhyme is wrong about two details and right
    about the important one.
  - It has been given so many coats that it can barely lift its arms, and it would like to
    trade one away.
  description: >-
    Waist-high, dressed in whatever it has most recently been given, and holding something
    out. The Kindly are always holding something out. They speak politely, in whatever language
    you used first, and they will wait a very long time for an answer.
  notes: >-
    Level 2 standard: no overrides. Morale 2 because it leaves at the first hurt; any hit
    makes it lone and hurt, and at morale 2 only snake eyes keeps it there.


    The numbers exist so a table that starts a fight has one to finish. They are not an invitation.
    It does not fight and has never fought, and nothing in this book costs a campaign more
    than attacking one.


    It opens every encounter with an offer, and the offer is always real. It never lies, never
    haggles twice, and never mentions the slight again, to you.


    Appears: alone, at a crossing, a threshold, or a place where something was lost.
  reskin_of: null
